<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali ibn Isa ibn al-Jarrah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/859">859</a>–<a href="/years/946">946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā ibn al-Jarrāḥ, the good vizier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali ibn Isa ibn al-Jarrah was an official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, born in Dayr Qunna into a family with a long history of government service, who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> in 913–917 and 927–928 and in effect also during the vizierate of Hamid ibn al-Abbas in 918–923. His career, which coincided with the terminal decline of the Abbasid state, was marked by a power struggle with his rival Abu'l-Hasan Ali ibn al-Furat and by frequent exile. Austere and a determined opponent of corruption, he made many enemies, but he was later remembered as the good vizier for his administrative talent and honesty. He died in 946 during the sack of Baghdad by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>.</p></div>
